=BE=D0=B3=D0=BB=D0=B0 =D0=B1=D1=8B =D0=B7=D0=BB=D0=BE=D1=83=D0=BF=D0=BE=D1=
=82=D1=80=D0=B5=D0=B1=D0=BB=D1=8F=D1=82=D1=8C.
			</div></div><div class=3D"para">
				More often than not, Debian developers use a higher-level program such =
as <code class=3D"command">debuild</code>; this runs <code class=3D"command=
">dpkg-buildpackage</code> as usual, but it also adds an invocation of a pr=
ogram that runs many checks to validate the generated package against the D=
ebian policy. This script also cleans up the environment so that local envi=
ronment variables do not =E2=80=9Cpollute=E2=80=9D the package build. The <=
code class=3D"command">debuild</code> command is one of the tools in the <s=
pan class=3D"pkg pkg">devscripts</span> suite, which share some consistency=
 and configuration to make the maintainers' task easier.
			</div><a id=3D"id-1.18.4.7.10" class=3D"indexterm"></a><a id=3D"id-1.18.=
4.7.11" class=3D"indexterm"></a><div class=3D"sidebar"><a xmlns=3D"" id=3D"=
sidebar.schroot"></a><div class=3D"titlepage"><div><div><p class=3D"title">=
<strong><span class=3D"emphasis"><em>QUICK LOOK</em></span> Building packag=
es in <span class=3D"emphasis"><em>chrooted</em></span> and <span class=3D"=
emphasis"><em>virtual</em></span> environments</strong></p></div></div></di=
v><a id=3D"id-1.18.4.7.12.2" class=3D"indexterm"></a><a id=3D"id-1.18.4.7.1=
2.3" class=3D"indexterm"></a><a id=3D"id-1.18.4.7.12.4" class=3D"indexterm"=
></a><a id=3D"id-1.18.4.7.12.5" class=3D"indexterm"></a><div class=3D"para"=
>
				Building a package on an existing system, like a workstation or server,=
 can pose several problems. Maybe the system has packages installed, that c=
onflict with the build requirements. Or maybe it has packages (libraries, h=
eaders, etc.) installed, that the project to build is not supposed to use -=
 e.g. from <span class=3D"distribution distribution">Testing</span>, <span =
class=3D"distribution distribution">Unstable</span>, or even from third par=
ties. Also it might have packages installed, which are actually required fo=
r the build, but are not listed in <code class=3D"filename">debian/control<=
/code>. It is therefore a good practice to build a packages in =E2=80=9Ccle=
an=E2=80=9C environments.
			</div><div class=3D"para">
				The <code class=3D"command">sbuild</code>, <code class=3D"command">pbui=
lder</code>, or <code class=3D"command">cowbuilder</code> programs (in the =
similarly named packages) allow building a Debian package in a <span class=
=3D"emphasis"><em>chrooted</em></span> environment. Each of them first crea=
tes a temporary directory containing the minimal system required for buildi=
ng the package (including the packages mentioned in the <span class=3D"emph=
asis"><em>Build-Depends</em></span> field). This directory is then used as =
the root directory (<code class=3D"filename">/</code>), using the <code cla=
ss=3D"command">chroot</code> command, during the build process.
			</div><a id=3D"id-1.18.4.7.12.8" class=3D"indexterm"></a><div class=3D"p=
ara">
				These tools allow the build process to happen in an environment that is=
 not altered by users' manipulations. This also allows for quick detection =
of the missing build-dependencies (since the build will fail unless the app=
ropriate dependencies are documented). Finally, it allows building a packag=
e for a Debian version that is not the one used by the system as a whole: t=
he machine can be using <span class=3D"distribution distribution">Stable</s=
pan> for its normal workload, and the chrooted environments can be running =
on the same machine using <span class=3D"distribution distribution">Unstabl=
e</span> for package builds. Using these tools, it is also possible to buil=
d for a different distribution, like <span class=3D"distribution distributi=
on">Ubuntu</span>.
			</div><div class=3D"para">
				<code class=3D"command">schroot</code>, <code class=3D"command">sbuild-=
shell</code>, or <code class=3D"command">pbuilder</code> allow running a co=
mmand or a login shell in a <span class=3D"emphasis"><em>chrooted</em></spa=
n> environment.
			</div><div class=3D"para">
				Sometimes it is not enough to build a package in a chrooted environment=
. This can be due to building for a different architecture where cross-comp=
ilation does not work, or due to the tools not being able to create a chroo=
ted environment for the desired target system. Then one can create virtual =
machines to build the package there. These setups are usually more complex =
then the tools mentioned above, and rarely needed, so we will only mention =
this possibility without going into much detail. Check out <a class=3D"xref=
" href=3D"https://debian-handbook.info/browse/ru-RU/stable/sect.virtualizat=
ion.html">=D0=A0=D0=B0=D0=B7=D0=B4=D0=B5=D0=BB&nbsp;12.2, =C2=AB=D0=92=D0=
=B8=D1=80=D1=82=D1=83=D0=B0=D0=BB=D0=B8=D0=B7=D0=B0=D1=86=D0=B8=D1=8F=C2=BB=
</a> for an introduction into this topic.
			</div><a id=3D"id-1.18.4.7.12.12" class=3D"indexterm"></a></div></div></=
